{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1155639-do-you-think-a-sin-eater-would-make-a-good-survival-horror-game","id":1155639,"title":"Do you think a Sin Eater would make a good survival horror game?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-11-03T20:53:38-07:00","id":1155639,"title":"Do you think a Sin Eater would make a good survival horror game?","content":"As in the Main Character is a Sin Eater.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1945-magicballz","name":"Magicballz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"175"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-03T22:35:43-07:00","id":1155651,"title":"","content":"Not particularly. Survival horror is based around the character having a tough time surviving and in a horror-oriented situation, but Sin-Eaters come back from death and are very powerful on their own.<br /> <br /> You could swing it, but you would need to stretch the way you would to make every game a horror game.<br /> <br /> Vampires are powerful, for instance, but they are drains on Vitae (ha ha). Every 2 Bashing or 1 Lethal requires a point of Vitae to heal, Even if you're starting against humans who can't overcome the fact that you're a corpse and don't receive more than Bashing from most injuries, that's still requiring Vitae for hit. You can balance it out by draining your attackers, but if you had multiple attackers that just means your opponents are going to get free attacks. If you had a situation where a vampire was in enemy territory and had to get out of it because Daysleep in that situation would equal Death with a capital Final, then you have a survival horror situation.<br /> <br /> Sin-Eaters could achieve a similar thing, but I think if you wanted to keep it in setting for them you would have to drop them into the Underworld. Even then, their powers are dramatically more powerful than vampires (not counting the high end of things, though I couldn't tell you how Sin-Eaters scale) and in the Underworld you would have many opportunities to recoup any Plasm.<br /> <br /> Mind this is just considering first edition, second seems to be going another way for it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-04T02:00:11-07:00","id":1155670,"title":"","content":"Well mages vs the abess is pretty frightening seeing lovecrafting horror the same about changlings and the true fae plus hobgoblins make pretty good standard foes and the mintel instability is horror material in itself plus there's also promethen and hunter and demen and even beast though i hate it so","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13426-ameraaaaaa","name":"Ameraaaaaa","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"320"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-04T05:09:36-07:00","id":1155697,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1155639-do-you-think-a-sin-eater-would-make-a-good-survival-horror-game?p=1155651#post1155651\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not particularly. Survival horror is based around the character having a tough time surviving and in a horror-oriented situation, but Sin-Eaters come back from death and are very powerful on their own.<br /> <br /> You could swing it, but you would need to stretch the way you would to make every game a horror game.<br /> <br /> Vampires are powerful, for instance, but they are drains on Vitae (ha ha). Every 2 Bashing or 1 Lethal requires a point of Vitae to heal, Even if you're starting against humans who can't overcome the fact that you're a corpse and don't receive more than Bashing from most injuries, that's still requiring Vitae for hit. You can balance it out by draining your attackers, but if you had multiple attackers that just means your opponents are going to get free attacks. If you had a situation where a vampire was in enemy territory and had to get out of it because Daysleep in that situation would equal Death with a capital Final, then you have a survival horror situation.<br /> <br /> Sin-Eaters could achieve a similar thing, but I think if you wanted to keep it in setting for them you would have to drop them into the Underworld. Even then, their powers are dramatically more powerful than vampires (not counting the high end of things, though I couldn't tell you how Sin-Eaters scale) and in the Underworld you would have many opportunities to recoup any Plasm.<br /> <br /> Mind this is just considering first edition, second seems to be going another way for it.</div> </div> </div> </div>It might work a bit if you bent the subject of the Horror - while Sin-Eaters keep coming back, at least in 1E, someone else died in their stead. If the SE (and the player, by proxy) actually cares about a bunch of NPCs, then each death mean another one of them dies.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-04T08:38:07-07:00","id":1155737,"title":"","content":"<i>Survival</i> horror? Not really. Sin-Eaters are probably the most survivable template in the game. Every gameline is made to work for horror of some kind, but <i>survival</i> horror specifically is kind of blue book\u2019s territory.<br /> <br /> What it is great for though is transitioning from a blue book game into a game line. If the human characters die, you can bring them back as Bound.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-04T12:37:53-07:00","id":1155818,"title":"","content":"What if a Sin-Eater wants to die? THe bound would keep bring him/her back?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1945-magicballz","name":"Magicballz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"175"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-04T13:20:02-07:00","id":1155833,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magicballz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1155639-do-you-think-a-sin-eater-would-make-a-good-survival-horror-game?p=1155818#post1155818\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What if a Sin-Eater wants to die? THe bound would keep bring him/her back?</div> </div> </div> </div>Interesting question. I don\u2019t know if the books really deal with this. Mostly because you really have to want to come back to become Bound in the first place. It\u2019s kind of like asking what if a ghost wants to pass on? If it wanted that, it probably wouldn\u2019t have become a ghost. Plus, a major theme of the game is celebrating life - getting a second chance allows you to appreciate it all the more. A suicidal Sin-Eater would be kind of counter to the game\u2019s themes. But it would be an interesting concept to delve into. I\u2019m tempted to say that yes, the Geist does bring them back, if only because there\u2019s more of a story to be told with that answer.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-04T13:55:29-07:00","id":1155846,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1155639-do-you-think-a-sin-eater-would-make-a-good-survival-horror-game?p=1155833#post1155833\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Interesting question. I don\u2019t know if the books really deal with this. Mostly because you really have to want to come back to become Bound in the first place. It\u2019s kind of like asking what if a ghost wants to pass on? If it wanted that, it probably wouldn\u2019t have become a ghost. Plus, a major theme of the game is celebrating life - getting a second chance allows you to appreciate it all the more. A suicidal Sin-Eater would be kind of counter to the game\u2019s themes. But it would be an interesting concept to delve into. I\u2019m tempted to say that yes, the Geist does bring them back, if only because there\u2019s more of a story to be told with that answer.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well the thing is, What if a Sin-eater got into a fight and lost almost all its limbs? I mean surely some would rather die than live like that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1945-magicballz","name":"Magicballz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"175"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-04T14:02:19-07:00","id":1155848,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magicballz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1155639-do-you-think-a-sin-eater-would-make-a-good-survival-horror-game?p=1155846#post1155846\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well the thing is, What if a Sin-eater got into a fight and lost almost all its limbs? I mean surely some would rather die than live like that.</div> </div> </div> </div>I believe a Geist can sever the connection if the host isn't able to do its thing. The geist is in it for a reason, after all, not generally to sit in a hospital bed. Presumably it can just find a new host.<br /> <br /> But if a Sin-Eater just commits suicide, like Charlaquin points out, the Bound can bring them back, I believe this is the subject of the SAS. A lot is going to depend on the geist involved, however.<br /> <br /> It's a lot like being Claimed, really. The end result is a bit different, but the host can leave. Thing is they have a reason not to, because while it's easy to say they'll just find another host, sometimes that requires a unique confluence of events, such as someone dying in a certain way. And in the meantime, the ephemeral entity is vulnerable to the things ephemeral entities are usually vulnerable to, in the case of ghosts, being sucked down the Underworld.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 11-04-2017, 05:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-04T14:06:21-07:00","id":1155850,"title":"","content":"It's indeed up to the Geist. It's brought up that a lot of Sin-Eaters really don't like being resurrected, but that generally a Geist will do it anyway unless the Sin-Eater has totally worn through their Synergy and even then some don't let go. That's how you get the Wretched.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-04T20:08:45-07:00","id":1155924,"title":"","content":"An example of a sin-eater who has to deal with a geist bringing him back to against his will is Jackie Estacado in the second time he dies in The Darkness video game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/904596-geniuses-and-gramarie\" target=\"_blank\">Genius templates</a>: Super Science Mini-Template for Demon: the Descent<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1337874-oracle-the-endbringers-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Oracle the Endbringers</a>: Time-Manipulator Fan-Splat","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","name":"milo v3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8331_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1505"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-04T20:49:58-07:00","id":1155930,"title":"","content":"The concept is somewhat addressed in one of the short fictions in the Geist corebook. The Sin Eater isn't suicidal, persay, but he has made his Geist promise not to resurrect him if he dies again because he knows that someone else will then be killed in his stead. Naturally, he gets shot by a rival and the Geist reneges on the promise.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5328-helmsderp","name":"HelmsDerp","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"652"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-08T02:41:55-08:00","id":1156938,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HelmsDerp</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1155639-do-you-think-a-sin-eater-would-make-a-good-survival-horror-game?p=1155930#post1155930\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The concept is somewhat addressed in one of the short fictions in the Geist corebook. The Sin Eater isn't suicidal, persay, but he has made his Geist promise not to resurrect him if he dies again because he knows that someone else will then be killed in his stead. Naturally, he gets shot by a rival and the Geist reneges on the promise.</div> </div> </div> </div>Why did the Geist reneges the promise?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1945-magicballz","name":"Magicballz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"175"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-08T05:05:11-08:00","id":1156969,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magicballz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1155639-do-you-think-a-sin-eater-would-make-a-good-survival-horror-game?p=1156938#post1156938\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why did the Geist reneges the promise?</div> </div> </div> </div>It recognises the Geist of the killer, and it wants the Sin-Eater to hunt them down.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}